  "notes": [
    "Gaia DR3 2690227738799604224: retained as a Galactic unbound candidate in the detailed analysis (T:p12-b2; T:p18-b1) and treated as the one non-bound case in the conclusion (T:p22-b2), although the abstract states that all stars are bound (T:p1-b12) and the authors caution that its low-SNR Gaia radial velocity may be spurious (T:p6-b2; T:p22-b2). Gaia DR3 3631125674577631104: included because the paper explicitly calls it an unbound candidate under the alternate StarHorse distance, while noting a large V_GC uncertainty (T:p27-b1); this candidate is not part of the primary Bailer-Jones-distance assessment.",
    "Gaia DR3 2690227738799604224: Table 2 heads the right ascension and declination columns as “deg ± mas”; I retained that unit. No individual distance or numerical bound/unbound probability is reported for this target. The paper identifies Gaia DR3 2690227738799604224 as the only unbound candidate (positive orbital energy; see also T:p12-b2 and T:p22-b2), but cautions that its Gaia radial velocity has low SNR (3.2) and that its high Galactocentric velocity may therefore be unreliable. The V_GC and R_GC entries are approximate readings from the target’s outlier position in Fig. 1, not values tabulated numerically in the text.",
    "Gaia DR3 3631125674577631104: Target Gaia DR3 3631125674577631104 appears in Table 2. Its independent spectroscopic radial-velocity column is blank in that table. Appendix A states that this star is an unbound candidate if the StarHorse distance is used, but with a large uncertainty in V_GC; no numerical V_GC, distance, galactocentric radius, or bound/unbound probability is reported for this star (T:p27-b1)."
  ]
